        <h1>H2 Economics Case Study: How to Score L3 Every Time</h1>
        <div class="blog-post-meta">
          <a class="blog-author" href="/about/" rel="author">By Jeremy Lim, Founder</a>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <time datetime="2026-05-08">8 May 2026</time>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <span class="blog-reading-time">7 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-post-body">
        <p>The case study paper is where many H2 Economics students lose marks they could have kept. The essay paper rewards depth on one theme. The case study asks for different skills: reading extracts quickly, handling data, drawing precise diagrams and evaluating in a structured way, all under real time pressure. At A-Worthy we teach the SHARP Method: See the question type, Hit the right framework, Apply it correctly, Refine through guided feedback, Practise from memory. At the Hit step, students use a framework built for this paper. Below is the five-step approach we teach for L3 answers, then a worked example.</p>

        <h2>Understanding what L3 actually requires</h2>
        <p>Start with how Cambridge markers award levels. An L1 answer shows basic knowledge: correct definitions and surface-level description. L2 requires application, so the student uses economic concepts in the specific context of the case material. At L3 the student also analyses and evaluates: explaining chains of cause and effect, weighing limitations and reaching a reasoned judgement.</p>
        <p>What separates L2 from L3 is <strong>evaluation</strong>. Many students can apply concepts to the data. Far fewer can judge how far that analysis holds, how much it matters, and where it stops working. L3 needs you to ask: "Under what conditions would this analysis break down? What assumptions am I making? How significant is this effect compared to others?"</p>

        <h2>Step 1: Read the extracts strategically</h2>
        <p>Don&rsquo;t read the case material from start to finish like a story. Read it in three passes instead:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>First pass (2 minutes):</strong> Skim all extracts and data tables to identify the country, time period and economic context (recession, inflation, trade dispute, policy change). Note the main theme: is this about market failure, macroeconomic policy, trade, or a mix?</li>
          <li><strong>Second pass (3 minutes):</strong> Read each question, then go back to the extracts and underline the specific data, quotes or statistics each question refers to. Write the question number next to each underlined section.</li>
          <li><strong>Third pass (ongoing):</strong> As you answer each question, re-read only the sections it needs. Don&rsquo;t spend time on material that has nothing to do with the question in front of you.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This stops the most common time-waster: reading the whole case three or four times because you can&rsquo;t remember where a piece of information was.</p>

        <h2>Step 2: Decode data tables and charts in 60 seconds</h2>
        <p>Every case study includes data: tables, charts or both. It&rsquo;s there because examiners expect you to use it, and an answer that ignores the data can&rsquo;t score above L2. Here&rsquo;s how to get the most out of it quickly:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Identify the trend:</strong> Is the variable increasing, decreasing or fluctuating? Over what time period?</li>
          <li><strong>Calculate the change:</strong> Use percentage change, not absolute numbers. "GDP fell by $2 billion" is less precise than "GDP contracted by 3.2%." Examiners reward students who work with the data rather than just quoting it.</li>
          <li><strong>Spot the anomaly:</strong> Is there a year or data point that breaks the trend? It&rsquo;s almost always relevant to a question. Examiners put anomalies there on purpose.</li>
          <li><strong>Compare across variables:</strong> If the table shows both unemployment and inflation, look for the relationship. Does one rise as the other falls? This signals a Phillips Curve or policy trade-off question.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>When you use data in your answer, quote the exact figure: "As shown in Table 2, the inflation rate rose from 1.2% in 2022 to 4.8% in 2023, an increase of 3.6 percentage points." That kind of precision is what markers look for at L3.</p>

        <h2>Step 3: Structure every answer with a Claim-Reason-Evidence-Evaluation cycle</h2>
        <p>This is the framework our students use at SHARP&rsquo;s Hit step for case study answers. Each part lines up with what Cambridge markers look for at each level:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Claim (L1):</strong> State your economic concept or definition. "An increase in government spending would lead to a rise in aggregate demand."</li>
          <li><strong>Reason (L2):</strong> Explain the cause-and-effect chain using economic reasoning. "The rise in government spending on infrastructure injects income into the circular flow. Construction workers receive wages, which they spend on consumption. This creates a multiplier effect as each round of spending generates further income and consumption."</li>
          <li><strong>Evidence (L2&ndash;L3):</strong> Tie your argument to the case material with specific data, quotes or context from the extracts. "Extract 3 states that the government announced a $5.6 billion infrastructure package, while Table 1 shows that the economy's marginal propensity to consume is approximately 0.7, suggesting a multiplier of approximately 3.3."</li>
          <li><strong>Evaluation (L3):</strong> Assess the significance, limitations, or conditions. "However, the effectiveness of the multiplier depends on the extent of spare capacity in the economy. Extract 2 notes that unemployment is at 2.1%, which is close to full employment. Under these conditions, the increase in aggregate demand is more likely to be inflationary than output-enhancing, as firms compete for scarce labour and bid up wages."</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Every question part you answer should follow this sequence. A 6-mark question might need one Claim-Reason-Evidence-Evaluation cycle. A 10- or 12-mark question needs two or three, each covering a different aspect of the question.</p>

        <h2>Step 4: Integrate diagrams that earn marks</h2>
        <p>A diagram in a case study answer does two jobs: it shows you understand the model, and it saves you words. A clean AD-AS diagram with AD shifting right says in seconds what would take a full paragraph of prose.</p>
        <p>Three rules for case study diagrams:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Label everything:</strong> Both axes, both curves (before and after the shift), the initial and new equilibrium points, and the direction of the shift. An unlabelled diagram earns zero marks.</li>
          <li><strong>Reference it in your text:</strong> "As shown in the diagram, the increase in government spending shifts AD from AD1 to AD2, resulting in a rise in real GDP from Y1 to Y2 and an increase in the general price level from P1 to P2." A diagram you draw but never mention earns zero marks.</li>
          <li><strong>Contextualise it:</strong> Adapt the generic model to the case. If the case is about Singapore, your diagram should reference Singapore-specific factors. If the question is about a supply shock, label the cause of the shift (for example, "rise in oil prices") on the diagram itself.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The most commonly tested diagrams in case studies are AD-AS (for macroeconomic policy questions), demand-supply (for market and price analysis), and market failure diagrams (for externalities and public goods). Practise drawing each one until you can produce a clean, fully labelled version in under 90 seconds.</p>

        <h2>Step 5: Write evaluations that lock in L3</h2>
        <p>Evaluation is the L3 skill, yet many students either skip it or tack on a vague line like "however, this depends on many factors." That earns nothing. Good evaluation follows one of four patterns:</p>

        <h3>The conditions evaluation</h3>
        <p>"This policy would be effective <strong>if</strong> the economy has sufficient spare capacity, <strong>but</strong> Extract 2 suggests unemployment is near its natural rate, which means the multiplier effect would be weakened by crowding out." This type names a specific condition, then uses the case material to assess whether that condition holds.</p>

        <h3>The magnitude evaluation</h3>
        <p>"While the subsidy reduces the cost of solar panels, the reduction from $15,000 to $13,500 represents only a 10% decrease — unlikely to be the decisive factor for households already deterred by payback periods of 8&ndash;10 years." This type uses the data to assess whether an effect is large enough to matter.</p>

        <h3>The time-horizon evaluation</h3>
        <p>"In the short run, the contractionary monetary policy will reduce aggregate demand and ease inflationary pressure. However, Table 3 shows that the economy is simultaneously facing a supply-side shock from rising commodity prices. Demand-side policies cannot address cost-push inflation in the long run — structural reforms to reduce energy dependence would be more effective." This type distinguishes between short-run and long-run effects and proposes an alternative.</p>

        <h3>The trade-off evaluation</h3>
        <p>"Imposing a tariff on imported steel would protect domestic jobs in the short term. However, downstream industries such as construction and automobile manufacturing would face higher input costs, potentially leading to greater job losses than those protected. The net employment effect depends on the relative size of the protected and downstream sectors." This type identifies and assesses a trade-off using economic reasoning.</p>

        <h2>Worked example walkthrough</h2>
        <p>Take this typical 8-mark question: <em>"With reference to the data, explain how a depreciation of the Thai baht would affect Thailand's current account balance."</em></p>
        <p>Here&rsquo;s how to structure the answer with Claim-Reason-Evidence-Evaluation:</p>
        <p><strong>Claim:</strong> A depreciation of the Thai baht would, in theory, improve Thailand's current account balance through the price competitiveness effect.</p>
        <p><strong>Reason:</strong> When the baht depreciates, Thai exports become cheaper in foreign currency terms, increasing the quantity demanded by foreign buyers. Simultaneously, imports become more expensive in baht terms, reducing the quantity demanded by Thai consumers and firms. Assuming the Marshall-Lerner condition holds (that is, the sum of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceeds one), the current account balance would improve.</p>
        <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> Table 2 shows that the baht depreciated by 12% against the US dollar between 2023 and 2024. Extract 1 notes that Thailand's key exports are electronics and agricultural products, both of which face elastic demand in global markets. This suggests the Marshall-Lerner condition is likely satisfied, and the depreciation would stimulate export revenue.</p>
        <p><strong>Evaluation:</strong> However, the improvement may not be immediate. The J-curve effect suggests that in the short run, the current account may worsen because existing import contracts are denominated in foreign currency and cannot be renegotiated immediately. Table 2 supports this: despite the 12% depreciation, Thailand's trade deficit widened from $3.1 billion to $4.8 billion in the first six months. Furthermore, Extract 3 mentions that 40% of Thailand's imports are raw materials and intermediate goods with few domestic substitutes, meaning import demand is price inelastic for these categories. The current account improvement, if it materialises, is likely to be partial and delayed.</p>
        <p>This answer moves through all four stages: definition (L1), application with reasoning (L2), case-specific evidence (L2&ndash;L3), and conditional evaluation with data support (L3). Notice that every claim is tied to the case material. No point is made in the abstract.</p>

        <h2>Time management for the case study paper</h2>
        <p>Paper 1 gives you two case studies of 30 marks each: 60 marks in 2 hours 30 minutes, or about 75 minutes per case study. A common mistake is spending too long on the early sub-parts, which tend to carry fewer marks, and rushing the parts worth the most at the end. Keep to a strict time plan:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Initial reading and annotation:</strong> 10 minutes per case study</li>
          <li><strong>2-mark parts:</strong> 4 minutes each</li>
          <li><strong>4-mark parts:</strong> 7 minutes each</li>
          <li><strong>6-mark parts:</strong> 10 minutes each</li>
          <li><strong>8- to 10-mark parts:</strong> 15 minutes each</li>
          <li><strong>Review:</strong> 5 minutes per case study</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Write the time next to each question before you start. This one habit stops you running out of time on the questions worth the most.</p>
        <p>In our H2 Economics classes, students write case study answers against the clock, and every week their work comes back with written, marker-style feedback. That way they can see which answers reach L3, which don&rsquo;t yet, and why.</p>
